Let A be a real square matrix of order m. Denote by σ(A) the spectrum of A, and by l(A) the minimum of the real parts of the eigenvalues of A. Call A positive stable if every eigenvalue of A has strictly positive real part. Fix a positive integer n. Consider the following classes of matrices:

(i) A_{n,k,t}: for positive integers n,k and t ∈ (0,1), the matrices of a specified parametric family of order n indexed by (k,t) (the family whose stability behavior is studied in the τ-matrix literature);
(ii) the class of GKK τ-matrices, i.e. real matrices all of whose principal minors are positive, which are weakly sign-symmetric, and for which every principal submatrix B satisfies l(B) ≥ l(A);
(iii) the class of GKK matrices, i.e. real matrices with all principal minors positive and that are weakly sign-symmetric;
(iv) the class of τ-matrices, i.e. real matrices with all principal minors positive such that for every principal submatrix B one has l(B) ≥ l(A).

For a class C among (i)–(iv), let C_n denote the matrices in C of order at most n. Question: Is it true that every matrix A ∈ C_n which is positive stable satisfies the spectral angle inequality
   |arg(λ − l(A))| ≤ π/2 − π/n  for every λ ∈ σ(A)?

